    "answer": {
      "short": "The $r$ cards must be chosen from the $n$ cards, giving $\\binom{n}{r}$ choices. The factor $r!$ counts arrangements of an already selected set.",
      "worked": "The first procedure selects a set of $k$ cards and designates an unordered subset of $r$ cards to give away. In the second procedure, those $r$ cards must first be selected from all $n$ cards, in $\\binom{n}{r}$ ways. Permuting them in $r!$ ways neither selects the cards nor counts the same outcomes.\n\nThen choose the remaining $k-r$ cards from the other $n-r$ cards. The correct identity is\n$$\\binom{n}{k}\\binom{k}{r}=\\binom{n}{r}\\binom{n-r}{k-r}.$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
